package cosmonic:agent@0.4.4;

/// Types shared by `spawn` (child agent sessions), `tool-discovery` and
/// `tool-loading` (toolsets in the caller's own workload), and the contract
/// every implementer of those interfaces keeps. It has no free functions:
/// importing it grants nothing, and each power is its own import, so a world
/// grants exactly the ones it names. Its one resource, `approval`, is served
/// by the one implementer that serves `spawn` and `tool-loading`. Unstable:
/// a consumer enables the `agent-spawn` feature (and `agent-run` and
/// `session-status`, whose types these use). Rationale and the decisions
/// behind each rule are in `docs/spawn/DESIGN-NOTES.md`.
///
/// "The implementer" is whatever serves those imports: a native host
/// plugin, a policy layer the host installs in front of it, or a test
/// double. "The caller" is the component the import is bound to.
///
/// One session per workload. These imports are served only to a workload
/// the host bound to exactly one session for its whole life; a host that
/// binds several sessions to one
/// workload answers `disabled`. Toolsets are components of the workload, so
/// a workload shared by sessions could not keep their ceilings, toolsets and
/// removals apart, and none of these imports takes a session handle.
///
/// Caller identity. Wherever these interfaces bind a caller (approval
/// digests, coalescing, spawn tickets, the pending-approval cap, the denial
/// cooldown, repeat tracking, the session ceiling, loaded toolsets, a
/// child's owner), they bind the workload incarnation (minted when the
/// workload is created and kept across rollouts and restarts), the
/// component's name within that workload (so siblings are different
/// callers), and the session id the workload is bound to. Never an instance
/// id. Namespace and workload name are display and audit metadata. A policy
/// layer must be installed by the host and pass the original caller through:
/// a guest component in front of the implementer would become the caller.
///
/// Reach. A request's read reach is the union, over the agent and every
/// toolset, of its read grants: volumes, secrets, interfaces the
/// implementer classifies as reading, and the model alias. Its egress reach
/// is the union of allowed hosts and interfaces classified as writing. A
/// read-write volume counts both ways. Tool annotations (`read-only` and the
/// rest in `tool-types`) are publisher claims and never feed reach,
/// admission or the ceiling.
///
/// Ceiling. A caller's ceiling has two parts, and they never mix:
///
/// - its own grants: the grants of the caller's own component (for a child,
///   its `spawn-request.grants`). A new grant fits when one of these
///   contains it. A volume fits a mount of the same storage, at the same
///   path within that storage or below it, and a read-write grant fits only
///   a read-write mount. An allowed host fits by the containment rules in
///   `docs/spawn/DESIGN-NOTES.md` (`*.github.com` contains `api.github.com`,
///   not `github.com`; a bare host contains that host on any scheme's
///   default port, never another port). A model alias fits one the caller
///   was granted. A grant whose reach the implementer cannot see or compare
///   does not fit.
/// - its toolset entries: the toolsets it holds, each at an exact digest and
///   admission snapshot (declared toolsets, a child's spawned toolsets, and
///   the session ceiling below). A toolset fits when it is an exact match
///   for an entry. An entry's reach is never treated as the caller's own,
///   so approving one toolset never lends its reach to another component.
///
/// Within the ceiling, results flowing up are not a leak: the caller could
/// have read the same data itself.
///
/// Session ceiling. When a person approves a toolset load for a session,
/// the approval adds that toolset to the session's ceiling as an entry keyed
/// to the caller, the `toolset-spec` digest and the admission snapshot it
/// was reviewed at. A later load of the identical toolset (after the host
/// set it aside or unbound it, a rollback, or a failed rollout) is a load
/// within the ceiling and needs no prompt.
///
/// - Re-checked at each use: the implementer resolves the request again,
///   compares it with the entry's snapshot and checks current policy, in the
///   step that admits it. Any difference leaves the toolset outside the
///   ceiling.
/// - Never widens: an entry admits its exact snapshot only, and only a
///   person's load approval adds one. A spawn approval adds none to the
///   caller's ceiling (see Children).
/// - Only the person removes. Removing a toolset from a session (the
///   person's action in the host's UI, never an import) drops its entry. The
///   implementer then withdraws everything that rests on that entry: a
///   pending approval for it resolves `expired`, a granted spawn ticket not
///   yet consumed that names it is void, a rollout adding it is not
///   published, it is unbound, a call to it in flight is cancelled, and any
///   descendant's admission that rested on it is withdrawn (its toolset
///   unbound, or the child `revoked`). Admissions resting on other entries
///   are untouched. The session's revocation generation, a counter advanced
///   by every removal, is how a step that started before a removal
///   notices one: on a mismatch it re-checks that its own entry still
///   exists, and proceeds if it does. A refused step answers `denied`. A
///   removal also starts the denial cooldown for that toolset in that
///   session, so the agent cannot prompt for it again at once.
/// - Session-scoped and audited: it lives in the session's state, moves with
///   the session between hosts, and ends with it. The host's UI lists every
///   entry, bound or not, so the person can remove any.
///
/// Attenuation. A caller may request more than its ceiling but cannot grant
/// it. The implementer is the sole grantor (by policy, an auditor the host
/// installs, or a person) and returns `needs-approval`. Within the ceiling
/// the implementer admits at once only toolsets and agents whose digest is
/// in the operator's catalog or matches a toolset entry; any other digest
/// goes to a grantor, since new code is new risk even when its reach is not.
///
/// Children. A child session's reach never exceeds what its ancestors hold
/// plus what a person approved for that exact child.
///
/// - The child agent component's own `grants` must fit the parent's own
///   grants, and its model alias one the parent was granted; otherwise the
///   spawn is `denied`. Reach the parent lacks goes in a toolset. An agent
///   `definition` from the catalog is expanded into its component, alias,
///   grants and toolsets, and admitted by the same rules.
/// - A child's toolset that fits the parent's ceiling is admitted, resting
///   on the parent's grant or entry it matched. One beyond it goes to a
///   grantor, and only if it fits the ceiling of every ancestor above the
///   parent (none, for a root parent); otherwise it is `denied`.
/// - A spawn approval is a one-time ticket for one child (one idempotency
///   key). It adds nothing to the parent's ceiling: the parent cannot load
///   the toolset, and no other child can use it. The child holds it as a
///   toolset entry bound to the spawn's digest, so it carries the exact
///   output, budget and powers the person approved.
/// - Re-checked as the tree changes. The implementer records, for each of a
///   child's grants and entries, the authority it rested on: an ancestor's
///   grant or entry, or the person's approval of this exact child. At each
///   run acceptance and each child load, it re-checks each against that
///   authority as it stands now and against current policy. An entry resting
///   on an approval of this child is checked against that approval (its
///   snapshot, and that the person has not removed the toolset) and against
///   the ancestors above the parent it was checked against when approved,
///   not against the parent's ceiling, which never held it. A toolset that
///   no longer fits is withdrawn, and a child whose own grants no longer fit
///   takes no more runs (`revoked`). A change takes effect at the next
///   acceptance or dispatch, never mid-call.
/// - A child's loads follow the same rule: beyond its ceiling but within
///   every ancestor's goes to a grantor, labelled with the child's place in
///   the tree; beyond any ancestor's is `denied`.
///
/// Toolsets are dispatched, never linked. A toolset is one component, with
/// its own grants, in the agent's own workload, that offers one or more
/// tools: a tool provider (it exports `cosmonic:agent/tools`) or a typed
/// tool (it exports its own interfaces). Its exports are reachable only
/// through the host's dispatch, never linked to the agent or a sibling, and
/// its imports are satisfied only by its own grants. A host whose runtime
/// would link a toolset's exports to a sibling refuses that toolset with
/// `invalid-request`. A toolset egresses as itself, with its own grants and
/// identity.
///
/// Authorship. A model can paraphrase anything it read, so redaction is not
/// a security boundary and a child's final reply is a declassification
/// channel by nature. Nothing else the caller sees carries child-authored
/// text: progress, errors, reasons, the outcome's `finish` and `model`, and
/// its counts are the implementer's own (it serves the child's `chat` and
/// meters it). A child's conversation is never observable through these
/// interfaces; a person sees it in the host's own view. A person who talks
/// to a child may say things that reach the parent in a later reply.
///
/// Steerable channels. A child still chooses how much work it does, which
/// way its run ends, whether an outcome exceeds `output.max-bytes`, and its
/// timing. Each is a narrow channel to the caller. Nothing here closes
/// covert or timing channels, and nothing here blocks prompt injection.
///
/// Credentials never pass through these interfaces. Models, secrets and
/// credential references are named; the implementer resolves them and keeps
/// values out of every agent's memory. A spawned agent's own component never
/// receives secrets.
///
/// Audit. The implementer records every admission (through the catalog,
/// a toolset entry or a grantor), every refusal, removal, withdrawal,
/// set-aside and unbind, each naming what it rested on.
///
/// Authority to decide. A spawned agent never holds approval authority: the
/// grantor is a person, or policy a person wrote. An auditor is installed by
/// host policy, never spawned.
@unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
interface spawn-types {
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use wasi:clocks/types@0.3.0.{duration};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use inference-types.{json};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use run-types.{run-outcome, run-counts};

    /// A component image pinned by content digest, so a moving tag cannot
    /// change what was approved.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record component-ref {
        /// An OCI reference such as `ghcr.io/acme/agents/coder:1.2.0`. For
        /// display; `digest` is authoritative. The caller chooses it, so the
        /// implementer shows a person its own catalog name for a catalogued
        /// digest, and otherwise shows this reference only after confirming
        /// the repository serves this digest (the tag only if it resolves
        /// to it now); a reference it cannot confirm is `invalid-request`.
        image: string,
        /// An OCI digest, `sha256:<hex>`.
        digest: string,
    }

    /// A named volume mounted into one component.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record volume-mount {
        /// The name of a volume the implementer knows, resolved in the
        /// caller's scope. Never a host path. Two workloads may use one name
        /// for different storage, so the implementer compares the storage,
        /// never the name, and an approval binds the storage it resolved to.
        volume: string,
        /// Where the component sees the volume.
        mount-path: string,
        /// True mounts the volume read-only. Read-write counts toward both
        /// read and egress reach.
        read-only: bool,
    }

    /// An interface granted to one component. The implementer, not the
    /// request, classifies it as reading, writing, or both.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record interface-grant {
        /// Fully qualified interface name with its version. Never a
        /// `cosmonic:agent` interface: those powers reach a component only
        /// through `agent-powers` or the workload's own manifest, so naming
        /// one is `invalid-request`.
        name: string,
        /// Interface-specific configuration, such as a bucket or topic.
        config: list<tuple<string, string>>,
    }

    /// What one component may touch. Empty grants nothing; nothing is
    /// inherited from the caller.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record grants {
        /// Hosts this component may reach.
        allowed-hosts: list<string>,
        /// Volumes mounted into this component.
        volumes: list<volume-mount>,
        /// Interfaces this component may import.
        interfaces: list<interface-grant>,
        /// Secret names the implementer delivers into this component's
        /// memory. Names only, never values. Refused on a spawned agent's own
        /// component; use `credentials` for authenticated egress.
        secrets: list<string>,
        /// Credential references: stable logical names, not secret data.
        /// The implementer resolves each through a credential binding the
        /// operator scoped to this workload, applies it to the component's
        /// outbound requests, and owns those authentication headers. The
        /// value never enters the component. An implementer without a
        /// credential broker treats every reference as beyond the ceiling.
        credentials: list<string>,
    }

    /// A toolset: one component, with its own grants, that offers one or
    /// more tools. Used by `spawn` for a child's toolsets and by
    /// `tool-loading` for a toolset the caller loads.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record toolset-spec {
        /// A DNS label: lowercase letters, digits and `-`, starting and
        /// ending with a letter or digit, at most 32 characters. Unique among
        /// one agent's toolsets. Display metadata and the prefix of a
        /// namespaced alias; never used for routing or authorization, which
        /// follow the toolset's component identity and digest.
        name: string,
        /// A tool provider (exports `cosmonic:agent/tools`) or a typed tool
        /// (exports its own interfaces).
        component: component-ref,
        /// What this toolset component may touch.
        grants: grants,
        /// The functions offered, by their own names; none offers every
        /// function the component has. Trims a large component to what a
        /// job needs, so it spends less of the model's function budget. A
        /// name the component does not have is `invalid-request`.
        functions: option<list<string>>,
        /// The operator's override of how this toolset's tools are offered.
        /// None takes the default: each tool keeps its own name if no alias
        /// in the session has it yet, and is otherwise offered as
        /// `<name>__<tool>`. Some(prefix) offers every tool as the prefix
        /// followed by its own name, so the empty string means the own names
        /// and `"gh__"` means `gh__search`. An alias already taken, or one
        /// that breaks model tool-name rules (only `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`, at most
        /// 64 characters), refuses the request with `invalid-request`; the
        /// implementer never renames it. In a caller's request the
        /// implementer refuses it unless its operator lets callers choose
        /// prefixes.
        alias-prefix: option<string>,
    }

    /// What may come back to the caller. Covered by the approval digest.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record output-spec {
        /// JSON Schema that `structured` must satisfy. None means reply
        /// parts only.
        schema: option<json>,
        /// Ceiling on the encoded outcome of one run, in bytes.
        max-bytes: u32,
        /// True returns the validated `structured` value and nothing else
        /// the child wrote: the implementer empties `reply`, and builds every
        /// other field of the outcome from its own records. Requires
        /// `schema`; without one the request is `invalid-request`. For
        /// reading untrusted content in quarantine. The implementer does not
        /// check that the schema has no free strings.
        structured-only: bool,
    }

    /// What a child may spend. Covered by the approval digest. None takes
    /// the implementer's default, which policy may cap. The implementer
    /// enforces it, not the child: it refuses a run past `max-runs`, ends a
    /// run at `max-duration`, and meters the child's model calls itself.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record budget {
        /// Runs the child may take: the number of runs accepted, the first
        /// one included.
        max-runs: option<u32>,
        /// Model calls the child may make in one run (`max_turns` in the
        /// OpenAI Agents SDK, the Claude Agent SDK and Goose). Passed to the
        /// child as `run-options.max-steps` and enforced by metering.
        max-steps: option<u32>,
        /// Tokens the child may spend across all runs and model calls: the
        /// sum of `usage.prompt-tokens` and `usage.completion-tokens`.
        max-total-tokens: option<u64>,
        /// Wall-clock budget, measured from spawn. Passing it mid-run ends
        /// the run with an outcome whose finish is `max-duration`, and ends
        /// the child's session.
        max-duration: option<duration>,
    }

    /// Powers a spawned child may receive. Empty grants none: a child never
    /// inherits its caller's powers, and receives a power only when the
    /// caller holds it too.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    flags agent-powers {
        /// The child may start child sessions of its own, within the tree
        /// limits.
        spawn,
        /// The child may call `tool-discovery.list`.
        tool-discovery,
        /// The child may load toolsets into itself, bounded by its
        /// ancestors' ceilings.
        tool-loading,
    }

    /// Which agent a child session runs.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant agent-source {
        /// A component targeting `cosmonic:agent/runnable-agent`, with the
        /// request's own model alias, grants and toolsets.
        component(component-ref),
        /// The name of an agent definition in the operator's catalog: its
        /// component, model alias, grants and toolsets are the
        /// definition's. The request's `model-alias` must be empty and its
        /// `grants` and `toolsets` must be empty, or the request is
        /// `invalid-request`. The definition is expanded into its component,
        /// alias, grants and toolsets and admitted by the same rules as a
        /// component request: a catalog entry is not a privilege. The
        /// admission snapshot resolves the name to the definition's
        /// content, so a changed definition is a new request.
        definition(string),
    }

    /// Where the host should run a child. A hint: the host may honour it or
    /// choose otherwise, and says where the child ran in `spawn.child`.
    /// Placement never changes reach: a child runs only where its admission
    /// snapshot resolves identically (the same storage behind its volumes,
    /// the same credential bindings), and a hint that cannot be honoured
    /// without changing reach is ignored.
    ///
    /// The host places a child on the parent's own host whatever the hint
    /// when its grants (or its toolsets') name a host that means different
    /// things in different places: loopback, link-local and private-range
    /// addresses, single-label names, and cluster-local names. The audit
    /// entry records where it placed the child.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant placement {
        /// The host chooses.
        any,
        /// The parent's own host.
        same-host,
        /// A host group the operator named.
        host-group(string),
        /// A zone or region the operator named.
        zone(string),
        /// Reserved for placements a later version names. As a caller's
        /// input it is `invalid-request`.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Everything needed to start a child agent session. Declarative: its
    /// toolsets are fixed at spawn, and it changes them only through
    /// `tool-loading`, when it holds that power. Construction is lenient;
    /// `spawn` validates and returns `invalid-request`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record spawn-request {
        /// The agent the child runs.
        agent: agent-source,
        /// A model alias, as `models.open` takes it: one the caller was
        /// granted (a child was granted only its own). Counts toward read
        /// reach.
        model-alias: string,
        /// The child's instructions. Caller free text: not covered by the
        /// approval digest and never shown in its summary.
        instructions: string,
        /// What the agent component itself may touch. Must fit inside the
        /// caller's own grants.
        grants: grants,
        /// The child's toolsets, in order (the order assigns aliases). Names
        /// must be unique.
        toolsets: list<toolset-spec>,
        /// What may come back, and how much.
        output: output-spec,
        /// What the child may spend over its lifetime.
        budget: budget,
        /// Powers the child receives.
        powers: agent-powers,
        /// Where to run it. None is `any`. Not covered by the approval
        /// digest, since it never changes reach.
        placement: option<placement>,
        /// For logs, audit and display, and returned by `spawn.children`.
        /// Never policy input, and not covered by the approval digest.
        labels: list<tuple<string, string>>,
    }

    /// Where a toolset stands in the caller's session.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant toolset-state {
        /// Admitted, and its rollout has not finished: not callable yet. The
        /// rollout has a deadline (the operator's, recommended two minutes);
        /// if it fails or passes it, the toolset leaves `loaded()` and an
        /// identical `load` tries again. It cannot be set aside until it is
        /// `offered`.
        loading,
        /// In the model's tool list: its definitions are offered and its
        /// tools are callable.
        offered,
        /// Bound and approved but set aside: out of the model's tool list to
        /// keep it within the function budget. A `load` of the same spec
        /// returns it to `offered` at once, with no rollout. Its aliases
        /// answer `unknown-tool` with how to bring it back.
        ready,
        /// A state not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// A grantor's decision on a request that needed approval.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant decision {
        /// Granted. For a load, the toolset joins the session ceiling now,
        /// and any identical load admits it. For a spawn, resend the
        /// identical request with the same key: the grant admits one child.
        granted,
        /// Denied, with an implementer-authored reason. Identical requests
        /// from the same caller answer `denied` for the host's denial
        /// cooldown and raise no new prompt.
        denied(string),
        /// No decision before the approval expired, or the implementer
        /// withdrew it (its admission snapshot no longer matches, or the
        /// person removed the toolset). Not a denial: an identical request
        /// raises a new approval.
        expired,
        /// A decision not captured above. Treat as denied.
        other(string),
    }

    /// An earlier admission of the same digest from the same caller, which
    /// makes a new approval a repeat.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record prior-admission {
        /// The id of the approval whose grant admitted it.
        approval-id: string,
        /// How long before this approval was raised it was admitted.
        admitted-ago: duration,
        /// True while the child that admission started has not ended.
        still-running: bool,
    }

    /// A request sent to a grantor. The approval is host state: it stays
    /// pending until the grantor decides or it expires, whether or not
    /// anyone holds this handle. Dropping the handle only stops waiting.
    ///
    /// Coalescing. Identical requests from the same caller (the same
    /// workload incarnation, component and session) while an approval is
    /// pending return the same approval (the same id, one prompt), and every
    /// waiter sees the same decision.
    ///
    /// Loads. A granted load approval adds the toolset to the session
    /// ceiling at once; there is no separate one-time ticket, and an
    /// identical load is admitted within the ceiling. The "already held"
    /// rule in `tool-loading` keeps concurrent identical loads to one
    /// rollout.
    ///
    /// Spawns. A granted spawn approval is a one-time ticket for one child,
    /// consumed atomically by the first resend's idempotency key; a resend
    /// with that key later returns the same child. Consuming it checks, in
    /// the same step, the grant's expiry (the operator's lifetime), a fresh
    /// snapshot, current policy, every ancestor's current ceiling, and that
    /// no removal since the grant touched a toolset it names. Coalesced
    /// waiters with other keys get a new approval, marked as a repeat
    /// (`get-repeat-of`). A spawn approval adds nothing to the caller's
    /// session ceiling: the approved toolsets become the child's own entries,
    /// bound to the spawn's digest, so the parent cannot load them and no
    /// other child, with any other output, can use them.
    ///
    /// Admission snapshot. Before raising an approval, the implementer
    /// resolves the request into an immutable snapshot: the storage behind
    /// each volume, the credential binding behind each reference (the binding
    /// and what it authenticates as and to, not the secret value, so rotation
    /// within a binding changes nothing), the secret each name resolves to,
    /// the model alias's provider, model and endpoint, an agent definition's
    /// content, the resource and classification of each interface grant,
    /// every executable digest, and every default the request left open
    /// (budget fields that are none, and the configured operator limits,
    /// never live counters). The person-facing approval UI shows it and the
    /// audit log records it; it never reaches the caller, whose summary
    /// carries at most an opaque, keyed fingerprint. Admitting re-resolves
    /// and compares; any difference spends a spawn ticket or voids a session
    /// ceiling entry, and the request asks again. While pending, a request
    /// whose names now resolve differently withdraws the stale approval (its
    /// decision becomes `expired`) and raises one new approval.
    ///
    /// After a denial (or `other`, or the person's removal of the toolset),
    /// matching requests answer `denied` with no prompt for the host's
    /// cooldown (recommended one hour). "Matching" is by reach, not by
    /// name: the same executable digests and resolved reach anywhere in the
    /// spawn tree, so a fresh child, or a renamed toolset or a changed
    /// `max-bytes`, cannot ask again at once. Pending approvals are capped
    /// per caller (recommended three) and per tree (recommended five), each
    /// operator-set and at least one; past either cap a request answers
    /// `over-limit` with scope `pending-approvals` and raises no prompt.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    resource approval {
        /// Implementer-issued id, to correlate with the approval UI and the
        /// audit log.
        @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
        get-id: func() -> string;

        /// `sha256:<hex>` over a domain tag naming the operation and its
        /// version (`cosmonic:agent/spawn-request@3` or
        /// `cosmonic:agent/toolset-spec@3`), a zero byte, the caller's
        /// workload incarnation, a zero byte, the caller's component name, a
        /// zero byte, the caller's session id, a zero byte, and the request
        /// in canonical JSON. Compare for equality only.
        ///
        /// It covers exactly the fields the summary shows: the whole
        /// `toolset-spec` for a load; every `spawn-request` field except
        /// `instructions`, `placement` and `labels` for a spawn.
        ///
        /// Normalization, before digesting and before the ceiling check:
        /// `allowed-hosts` entries are parsed, scheme and host lowercased, an
        /// internationalized host converted to its A-label form, one trailing
        /// dot stripped, and a default port (80 for `http` and `ws`, 443 for
        /// `https` and `wss`) dropped; an entry that does not parse is
        /// `invalid-request`. Set lists (`allowed-hosts`, `secrets`,
        /// `credentials`, `volumes`, `interfaces`, `functions`) drop exact
        /// duplicates and are sorted by the UTF-8 bytes of each element's
        /// canonical JSON; two different elements with one key (two volumes
        /// at one `mount-path`, two interface grants with one `name`) are
        /// `invalid-request`. `toolsets` keeps its order. Names, digests and
        /// paths are compared as given.
        ///
        /// Each WIT value as JSON, serialized by RFC 8785 (JCS): a record is
        /// an object with every field, named as in this WIT; a string, bool
        /// or u32 is itself; u64 and `duration` are strings of decimal digits;
        /// an option is `null` or the value; a list is an array, and
        /// `list<tuple<string, string>>` is an object (a repeated key is
        /// `invalid-request`); flags are a sorted array of set names; a
        /// variant is its case name, or a one-member object `{"case":
        /// payload}`; `json` is embedded as parsed JSON.
        @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
        get-request-digest: func() -> string;

        /// Implementer-authored text for the person who decides: what is
        /// declassified ("results of this agent, schema S, at most N bytes,
        /// return to <caller>"), that a toolset joins the session ceiling
        /// (for a load) or is for this one child only (for a spawn), what a
        /// load would set aside, and where the requesting session sits in the
        /// tree. Built only from values the implementer validated: catalog
        /// names, confirmed image references, and caller-chosen names shown
        /// as quoted identifiers limited to `[a-z0-9-_./:]`. Free text the
        /// caller wrote never appears in it. A repeat opens by saying
        /// so. It never carries resolved infrastructure detail.
        @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
        get-summary: func() -> string;

        /// Some when the same caller had a spawn with this digest admitted
        /// within the repeat window (the operator's; recommended one hour,
        /// extended while that child has not ended): the most recent such
        /// admission. Only spawns set it.
        @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
        get-repeat-of: func() -> option<prior-admission>;

        /// Resolves when the grantor decides. Dropping the future stops
        /// waiting and does not withdraw the request.
        @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
        wait-for-decision: async func() -> decision;
    }

    /// Which implementer limit a request would exceed.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant limit-scope {
        /// Child sessions with a run in progress at once in one spawn tree
        /// (the root session and every descendant). Clears when one ends.
        /// Default 20.
        concurrent-agents,
        /// Nesting depth, where a child of a session a person or trigger
        /// started is at depth 1. Does not clear. Default 3.
        depth,
        /// Child sessions spawned in total by one spawn tree, ended ones
        /// included. Does not clear. Default 500, so the idempotency keys
        /// a tree has used stay bounded.
        total-agents,
        /// Memory the implementer reserves for a child.
        memory,
        /// Toolset components bound in one agent's workload at once, set
        /// aside ones included. Clears when one is unbound. Default 16.
        loaded-toolsets,
        /// Functions offered to the model at once, when nothing can be set
        /// aside to make room. The budget comes from the model binding.
        offered-functions,
        /// Approvals pending at once, per caller and per spawn tree. Always
        /// set. Clears when one is decided or expires.
        pending-approvals,
        /// Child sessions not yet ended in one spawn tree. Always set.
        /// Clears when one ends. Default 50.
        live-sessions,
        /// A scope not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// An implementer limit a request would exceed.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record over-limit {
        /// Which limit.
        scope: limit-scope,
        /// The limit's value, where the implementer reports it.
        limit: option<u64>,
    }

    /// Why a spawn admitted no child, or a load added no toolset. No child
    /// session was created and no toolset became callable. A component a
    /// failed rollout started may have run its own start-up code; its
    /// effects are not rolled back. Detail strings are implementer-authored
    /// and diagnostic only.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant admission-error {
        /// The operator has not enabled this power on this host, or did not
        /// grant it to this caller. Permanent for this caller: adapt rather
        /// than retry.
        disabled,
        /// A policy or a person refused, or the request reaches beyond what
        /// the caller's place in the tree allows. A different request may
        /// succeed. Also the answer, with no prompt, during the cooldown
        /// after a person denied the same request.
        denied(string),
        /// Sent to a grantor. Wait for the decision, then resend the
        /// identical request. Returned at once: a pending approval never
        /// holds the caller's run.
        needs-approval(approval),
        /// An implementer limit would be exceeded.
        over-limit(over-limit),
        /// Malformed: a malformed digest, a duplicate toolset name, a name
        /// already held by a different toolset, an unknown model alias,
        /// definition or function, a component that does not target the
        /// right world.
        invalid-request(string),
        /// The implementer could not serve the call, or the rollout failed
        /// and left the caller's toolsets as they were. Retry may succeed.
        unavailable(string),
        /// A failure not captured above. Treat as permanent. Diagnostic
        /// detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Why a run is paused. Implementer-authored.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant blocked-reason {
        /// A tool call or a load waits on a person or the auditor.
        tool-approval,
        /// Policy holds the outcome until a person or the auditor releases it.
        outcome-release,
        /// A reason not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Contentless progress. The implementer coalesces frames and never
    /// blocks on an unread stream.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant progress {
        /// The child is working.
        working,
        /// The run is paused.
        blocked(blocked-reason),
        /// Cancellation was requested and the child has not stopped yet;
        /// `spawn.send` answers `busy` until it has.
        cancelling,
        /// Cumulative counters for this run so far, from the implementer's
        /// own metering.
        counts(run-counts),
        /// A frame not captured above. Safe to ignore.
        other(string),
    }

    /// A budget item that can leave a child unable to take another run.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant budget-item {
        /// `budget.max-runs` runs were accepted.
        max-runs,
        /// `budget.max-total-tokens` were spent.
        max-total-tokens,
        /// `budget.max-duration` passed.
        max-duration,
        /// An item not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Why a child's run did not start. Nothing reached the child. Not the
    /// `run` export's `start-error`: this one adds what only an implementer
    /// that holds a budget and a session tree knows, and never says
    /// `duplicate`, since the implementer mints the child's run ids.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant start-error {
        /// A run is in progress, or a cancelled one has not stopped yet.
        busy,
        /// No session with that id is a direct child of the caller.
        unknown,
        /// The child's admission no longer fits its ancestors' current
        /// ceilings or policy, so it takes no more runs.
        denied(string),
        /// Accepting the run would exceed a tree limit
        /// (`concurrent-agents`). May clear when another run ends.
        over-limit(over-limit),
        /// A budget item is spent; the child takes no more runs.
        exhausted(budget-item),
        /// The message cannot be taken. A different message may succeed.
        invalid-input(string),
        /// The child's session has ended (by `end`, its `max-duration`, or
        /// its parent's end) and will never accept another run.
        ended,
        /// The run could not be accepted now (no host could take it, or the
        /// session store could not be reached). Retry may succeed.
        unavailable(string),
        /// A failure not captured above. Treat as permanent. Diagnostic
        /// detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Why a child's run failed after it started. Detail strings are the
    /// implementer's, never the child's. The child's own typed errors reach
    /// the caller only as `failed`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant run-error {
        /// The run was cancelled (`spawn.cancel` or `spawn.end`).
        /// Cooperative: effects caused before the child stopped are not
        /// rolled back.
        cancelled,
        /// The outcome failed `output.schema` or exceeded `output.max-bytes`.
        output-rejected(string),
        /// A person or the auditor declined to release the outcome.
        withheld,
        /// The host running the child went away mid-run (its lease in the
        /// session store lapsed). Effects before that are not rolled back.
        /// The child's session survives; a later `send` starts a new run.
        host-lost(string),
        /// The child failed. Details are in the host's view of its session.
        failed(string),
        /// The implementer could not finish serving the run.
        unavailable(string),
        /// A failure not captured above. Treat as permanent. Diagnostic
        /// detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// A child's run that has started: contentless progress, then the
    /// outcome. Unlike the `run` export's stream, the child's text and steps
    /// never reach the caller. The stream closes before the outcome
    /// resolves. Dropping the stream or the future only stops listening: a
    /// child may run on another host and outlive the caller's store, so only
    /// `spawn.cancel` or `spawn.end` cancels, and `spawn.wait` collects the
    /// outcome later by run id. With `output.structured-only`, the outcome's `reply` is
    /// empty and every other field is the implementer's own.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    type started-run = tuple<stream<progress>, future<result<run-outcome, run-error>>>;
}
